Plain-language summary (informative, not part of the license terms):

  Synesis is free software under the AGPLv3: if you modify Synesis and
  distribute it or run it as a network service, you must share your
  changes to Synesis under the AGPLv3.

  Your research data is yours. The files you feed to Synesis and the JSON,
  CSV, Excel, graph, and dataset outputs it produces from them are NOT
  covered by the AGPLv3 and are NOT subject to any copyleft obligation
  toward Synesis. You may license and use your outputs however you wish.

  This holds even when an output carries Synesis's own runtime pieces —
  for example, the interactive knowledge-graph HTML that Synesis generates
  bundles Synesis's own JavaScript and CSS so it can render. You can still
  publish or sell that HTML under any license you choose. What you may NOT
  do is copy Synesis's source code out of Synesis by hand and then claim
  this exception to strip the AGPLv3 from it.

  This is the same principle that lets code compiled with GCC (which links
  in GCC's own runtime), or parsers generated by Bison, be released under
  any license the author chooses.
